# Configuring another node's radio, over the mesh.
#
# The Settings tab looks exactly the same whichever radio it is describing - that is what makes
# the feature work, and it is why the client has to say which one somewhere else. This scene is
# that saying: the banner on every screen, the two sections that state it in rows, and the
# confirm sheet, which is the one place a banner cannot reach.
#
# `remote <name>` stands in for the press that gets here, which is a row on a node's own card.
# What is worth looking at is not that press; it is every screen afterwards.

scene demo
scale 4
delay 220

config
tab nodes
hold 900

# Pointed at a repeater. The banner costs rows and takes them, because this has settled and
# stays true until somebody comes back - and because a reader who misses it saves a setting onto
# somebody else's radio.
remote Hill repeater
hold 1800

# It follows across the tabs, because it is not about any of them. What it is about is what the
# next press will do.
tab messages
hold 1200
tab radio
hold 1200

# Into the Settings tab, where every section now describes that radio. The banner is still up:
# nothing in the section list says which radio it is a list for.
tab settings
hold 1400

# About radio, which does. The heading and the node lead the section, the way back sits under
# them, and the banner stands down - a banner pointing at a screen that states the same thing in
# more detail is noise on that screen. The node number is the target's, and the reboot count is
# gone: MyNodeInfo is what a radio tells the client attached to it.
key down 1
key a
hold 2200

# Back out, and it returns.
key b
hold 1200

# Radio actions. The same pair leads this section, because this is the list where getting the
# answer wrong takes somebody else's repeater down rather than merely writing to it.
key down 13
key a
hold 1800

# The reboot, confirmed. A modal owns the body, so the banner is gone at exactly the moment the
# question is put - and the sheet names the node itself instead.
key down 1
key a
hold 2600
